"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][29]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M29-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M29-L1 – Sensors, Perception and Coordinate Frames<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Robotics and Autonomous Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18, M22, M25.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Relate sensor measurements in\ntime and space, calculate rigid transformations and account for\nmeasurement uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A measurement is not a state<\/h2>\n<p>An autonomous transport trolley needs to recognise a loading station\nand determine its position relative to a map. Wheel encoders measure\nwheel rotation; an inertial measurement unit (IMU) measures angular\nvelocity and specific force; a camera records brightness values; and\nLiDAR estimates distances using emitted light signals. No single sensor\nprovides the complete state without a model. Encoders can be wrong when\nwheels slip, cameras are sensitive to lighting, and reflective surfaces\ncan distort range measurements. A measurement model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=h(x)+v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlinks the unknown state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the observation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand measurement noise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalibration determines, among other things, the parameters of this\nmapping and the sensor’s mounting pose on the vehicle. Timestamps must\nrefer to a common time base: at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na delay of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalready corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.12\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof travel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reference frames and transformations<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001691_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A 90-degree rotation and translation map a local robot point into a right-handed world coordinate system.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A 90-degree rotation and translation map\na local robot point into a right-handed world coordinate\nsystem.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Transforming points from robot to world\ncoordinates.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We choose a right-handed plane with world coordinates W and robot\ncoordinates B. The pose comprises a translation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=(3,2)^\\top\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an orientation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=90^\\circ=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe local vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_B=(2,1)^\\top\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a detected point relative to the robot. The rotation maps\nB-coordinates to W-coordinates:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\theta)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta&amp;-\\sin\\theta\\\\\\sin\\theta&amp;\\cos\\theta\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad p_W=R p_B+t.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R p_B=(-1,2)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_W=(2,4)^\\top\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe inverse mapping is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_B=R^\\top(p_W-t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^\\top R=I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn homogeneous coordinates,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>R<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>t<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{WB}=\\begin{pmatrix}R&amp;t\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncombines rotation and translation; in 2D the bottom row contains two\nzeros and a one. If sensor S has a fixed mounting pose relative to the\nvehicle,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{WS}=T_{WB}T_{BS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe rightmost transformation acts first. In 3D,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\in SO(3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\in SE(3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand rotations generally do not commute. Conventions such as REP-103\nsimplify integration but do not replace checking a sensor’s actual axis\ndefinitions.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>R<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>R<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>t<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(w,[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(R.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>(w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>t),p)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>C<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>R<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>np.diag([<span class=\"fl\">.04<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>R.T<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(C,np.diag([<span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.04<\/span>]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(w,C.diagonal())<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Uncertainty and data quality<\/h2>\n<p>For a known pose, measurement covariance transforms according to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma_W=R\\Sigma_B R^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA local variance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong x and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.01<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.01\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong y swap their axis assignments under the rotation above. If the\npose itself is uncertain, its covariance and any cross-covariances must\nalso be taken into account, for example through the Jacobian of the\ncomplete transformation. A short range reading does not automatically\nimply an obstacle: plausibility checks, sensor status and measurement\ntime are all part of data processing. When measurements conflict, a\nrobotic system must not simply select whichever reading supports its\npreferred route.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> A sensor is mounted\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin front of the robot without any additional rotation. It reports a\npoint at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0)^\\top\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin its sensor frame. Where is the point in world coordinates for the\npose above?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> In the robot frame it lies at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.5,0)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRotation and translation yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,3.5)^\\top\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mounting offset must be applied before the transformation to world\ncoordinates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/nu-gm-book-resource/\">Lynch\nand Park (2017): Modern Robotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · chapter overview and descriptions\nfor Chapters 2, 3, 10 and 11 opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://reps.openrobotics.org/rep-0103/\">Foote and Purvis:\nREP-103 – Standard Units of Measure and Coordinate Conventions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nconventions and SI units opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262201629/probabilistic-robotics/\">Thrun,\nBurgard and Fox (2005): Probabilistic Robotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · publisher page\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M29-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M29-L2 – State Estimation and Sensor Fusion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Robotics and Autonomous Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18, M22, M25.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a Bayesian filter\nmodel, calculate a Kalman measurement update and explain limitations of\ncombining sensors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observing a moving system<\/h2>\n<p>A trolley moves along a corridor. Its position is not directly known\nbut estimated from a motion model and measurements. A state vector may\ncontain position, orientation, velocity and sensor drift. The discrete\nlinear model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_k=F x_{k-1}+B u_k+w_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_k=H x_k+v_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the control input,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe process noise and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe measurement noise. The covariances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribe, among other things, the reliability of the motion model and\nsensor. Under linear Gaussian assumptions, a Kalman filter tracks the\nmean and covariance of the state distribution. This is not generally\nsufficient for nonlinear or multimodal distributions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Prediction and measurement update<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001698_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Gaussian distributions depict prior estimate, measurement, and a narrower fused position estimate.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Gaussian distributions depict prior\nestimate, measurement, and a narrower fused position\nestimate.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Fusion of prediction and sensor measurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The prediction is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x^-=F\\hat x+B u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-=FPF^\\top+Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe innovation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=z-H\\hat x^-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the difference between the measurement and its predicted value. It\nfollows that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=HP^-H^\\top+R,\\qquad K=P^-H^\\top S^{-1},\\qquad \\hat x^+=\\hat x^-+Kr.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For the covariance, the numerically robust Joseph form is useful:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^+=(I-KH)P^-(I-KH)^\\top+KRK^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsider a scalar teaching example: the trolley is predicted to be at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn independent distance measurement, converted into a world position,\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=4/5=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe corrected estimate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11.6\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand its variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic compares the two input densities with the narrower fused\ndensity. In this model the more precise measurement receives greater\nweight without completely discarding the prediction.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x,P,z,R<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">10.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>K<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>P<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(P<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>R)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> post<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>K<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(z<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> var<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>K)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>P<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>K<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>K<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>R<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose([K,post,var],[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">11.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>nis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(z<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(P<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>R)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(nis,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(post,var,nis)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nonlinearity and faulty measurements<\/h2>\n<p>The Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) linearises motion and measurement\nmodels around the current estimate. An Unscented Kalman Filter uses\nselected sigma points to approximate transformed moments; particle\nfilters represent a distribution through weighted samples and can model\nmultiple possible locations. The choice depends on the dynamics,\ndimensionality, computing budget and observability. Shared error\nsources—for example, two algorithms using the same camera images—make\nmeasurements correlated. Treating them as independent can lead to\nunderestimating uncertainty. For diagnosing errors, the normalised\ninnovation squared\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^\\top S^{-1} r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be examined. Under suitable model assumptions it follows a\nchi-squared distribution with degrees of freedom equal to the\nmeasurement dimension. An unusual value can indicate an outlier,\nincorrect association or an unsuitable model; it is not, on its own,\nevidence of safety.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Fuse the same prediction with an independent\nmeasurement of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhaving variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=4/(4+4)=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x^+=11\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^+=2\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEqually precise inputs receive equal weight. Reusing the same\nmeasurement twice, however, does not provide independent additional\ninformation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262201629/probabilistic-robotics/\">Thrun,\nBurgard and Fox (2005): Probabilistic Robotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · publisher page\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.05830\">Cadena et al. (2016):\nPast, Present, and Future of Simultaneous Localization And Mapping<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nintroduction and Section II read selectively<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://underactuated.mit.edu/\">Tedrake: Underactuated\nRobotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · contents overview of Policy Search and Feedback\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M29-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M29-L3 – Localisation, Mapping and SLAM<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Robotics and Autonomous Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18, M22, M25.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish localisation from\nmapping and calculate the effect of loop closure in a pose graph.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Map, pose and observation<\/h2>\n<p>When a map is already known, localisation estimates the current pose\nrelative to it. When the complete trajectory is known, a mapping method\ncan build a map of the surroundings from measurements. SLAM\n(Simultaneous Localisation and Mapping) combines both tasks: positions,\norientations and map features are estimated jointly. Two-dimensional\noccupancy grids can, for example, represent the probability of occupancy\nby an obstacle for each cell; landmark maps contain selected\nidentifiable features. A semantic map adds meanings such as door or\npassageway. An unknown cell must not be treated as traversable free\nspace.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Pose graph and loop closure<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001704_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Odometry estimates 0, 2, and 0.4 metres are corrected to 0, 1.87, and 0.13 metres after a loop closure.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Odometry estimates 0, 2, and 0.4 metres\nare corrected to 0, 1.87, and 0.13 metres after a loop\nclosure.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Loop closure in a pose graph.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Odometry accumulates motion increments, so even small errors\naccumulate. Recognising a previously visited place can provide an\nadditional relative pose measurement. In a pose graph, variables\nrepresent poses and edges represent relative observations. The following\ndeliberately one-dimensional example fixes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nwithout this constraint, the entire coordinate frame could be translated\narbitrarily. Two odometry steps measure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+2.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.6\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOdometry alone puts the second pose at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn independent place-recognition measurement reports that the endpoint\ncoincides with the starting point (relative measurement 0). With equal\nmeasurement variances, minimising the weighted sum of squared residuals\nreduces here to<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_{x_1,x_2}(x_1-2)^2+(x_2-x_1+1.6)^2+x_2^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>All residuals have units of metres; a common weight in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas been omitted. Differentiating and setting the derivatives to zero\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x_1-x_2=3.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-x_1+2x_2=-1.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsequently\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8667<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=1.8667\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1333<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=0.1333\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLoop closure corrects both poses, but finite measurement uncertainty\nmeans it does not enforce an exact coincidence.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.lstsq(A,b,rcond<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">None<\/span>)[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>A<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(x,[<span class=\"dv\">28<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(A.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>r,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(r<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>r,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">75<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(x,r<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>r)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Front end, back end and false associations<\/h2>\n<p>A front end processes sensor data and associates observations with\nexisting features. The back end estimates the variables under the\nresulting geometric and probabilistic constraints. In 2D and 3D,\nrotations and distance models are nonlinear; iterative optimisation with\nsparse matrices is commonly used. An incorrectly recognised loop closure\ncan severely distort a map, especially in similar-looking corridors.\nGeometric verification, robust cost functions and checks of temporal\nconsistency help but cannot guarantee correct association. Map ageing as\nan environment changes and sensor occlusion are further limitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> What does the model produce without the\nloop-closure edge? Why should a place-recognition measurement not be\nassigned infinite weight?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> Both odometry residuals can then be zero\nsimultaneously:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=0.4\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn infinitely weighted and possibly incorrect place-recognition\nmeasurement would force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand distort the other measurements. Relative confidence must be\nrepresented through the uncertainties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262201629/probabilistic-robotics/\">Thrun,\nBurgard and Fox (2005): Probabilistic Robotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · publisher page\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.05830\">Cadena et al. (2016):\nPast, Present, and Future of Simultaneous Localization And Mapping<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nintroduction and Section II read selectively<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M29-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M29-L4 – Planning, Control and Model Predictive Approaches<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Robotics and Autonomous Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18, M22, M25.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish a path, a trajectory\nand feedback control, and derive a constrained predictive controller in\na teaching model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a goal to executable motion<\/h2>\n<p>A destination alone does not specify how to move. A geometric path\ndescribes positions in space without assigning times; a trajectory\nadditionally specifies time, speed or other state variables. A global\nplanner uses a map and might search for a low-cost route with A*. A\nlocal planner adapts motion to current obstacles and physical\nconstraints. For a vehicle with nonzero dimensions, obstacles must be\nassessed against the vehicle’s whole footprint: a collision-free centre\npoint is not enough. A feedback controller compares the measured state\nwith a desired state and generates control inputs to reduce deviations.\nThese layers typically operate at different update rates and may impose\nconstraints on one another.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>One step of model predictive control<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001651_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A state approaches target 4 while the actuator magnitude remains no greater than 1.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A state approaches target 4 while the\nactuator magnitude remains no greater than 1.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Control trajectory with bounded actuator.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Model Predictive Control (MPC) optimises a sequence of control inputs\nover a finite prediction horizon, subject to dynamic, state and input\nconstraints. Only the first input is applied; the calculation is\nrepeated after the next measurement. A normalised teaching model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{k+1}=x_k+u_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses dimensionless quantities to describe motion along one axis. The\ntarget position is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe initial position is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the input bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u|\\le 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a horizon of one step, we minimise<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(u)=(x_0+u-x^*)^2+0.5u^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Differentiation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3u-6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith the unconstrained solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder the input bound, the unique minimum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nleading to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRe-optimising yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_3=11/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic shows this sequence and its approach to the goal. This\none-step model does not capture full vehicle dynamics; real systems\nrequire, for example, constraints on acceleration, steering angle,\ncollisions and delays.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> path<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[x]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> commands<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(np.clip((<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    commands.append(u)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> x<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> path.append(x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(path[:<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(path[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">map<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>,commands))<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(path)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Horizon, feasibility and fallback<\/h2>\n<p>Longer horizons allow more anticipatory decisions but require more\ncomputing time. An objective function may combine distance from the\ntarget, energy, jerk and risk-related costs. Hard constraints must be\ndistinguished from soft penalties: a collision penalty with a finite\nweight does not categorically rule out collisions. The optimisation\nproblem may be infeasible because the constraints conflict, or the\nsolver may exceed its time limit. A controlled fallback mechanism must\ndetect such cases and respond within verified operational limits. Robust\nMPC takes defined model uncertainties and disturbances into account;\nstability and recursive feasibility are additional properties, not\nautomatic consequences of calling a method MPC.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Start at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=3.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same model. What is the next control input, and how large is\nthe new deviation?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(4-3.4)/1.5=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich lies within the bound. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=3.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the remaining deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout saturation, the error ratio in this example is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper step.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/nu-gm-book-resource/\">Lynch\nand Park (2017): Modern Robotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · chapter overview and descriptions\nfor Chapters 2, 3, 10 and 11 opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://underactuated.mit.edu/trajopt.html\">Tedrake:\nUnderactuated Robotics – Trajectory Optimization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching chapter\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://underactuated.mit.edu/\">Tedrake: Underactuated\nRobotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · contents overview of Policy Search and Feedback\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M29-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M29-L5 – Robot Learning and Sim-to-Real Transfer<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Robotics and Autonomous Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18, M22, M25.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare learning strategies in\nrobotics and quantitatively examine the effect of varying simulation\nparameters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning in a feedback loop<\/h2>\n<p>A robot may learn a control strategy from demonstrations (imitation\nlearning), estimate a dynamical relationship from measurements (system\nidentification), or improve a policy through sequences of actions and\nfeedback (reinforcement learning; see M22). With imitation learning,\ndeviations from the states observed in demonstrations can lead to\nfurther errors; with reinforcement learning, behaviour and risks depend\non the reward function, exploration and permissible action space.\nLearning therefore does not automatically replace planning, state\nmonitoring or an independent safety function. Hardware trials consume\ntime and energy and cause wear; some failures are irreversible.\nSimulation offers a way of testing many scenarios without immediate\nphysical consequences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variation rather than an idealised simulation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001701_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Error curves compare a nominally trained policy against variability-trained alternatives as gain changes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Error curves compare a nominally trained\npolicy against variability-trained alternatives as gain\nchanges.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: How simulation variability affects the policy.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The sim-to-real gap is the difference between simulated and real\nobservations or dynamics. Domain randomisation varies, for example,\nfriction, lighting, camera parameters and mass during training. To\nillustrate the idea, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=a u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe a dimensionless linear motion with uncertain gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe target value is 1. We seek a constant command\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat minimises the empirical mean squared error for three equally\nweighted simulated gains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.6;1;1.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msubsup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>3.32<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.9036<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(u)=\\frac13\\sum_{i=1}^3(a_i u-1)^2,\\qquad u^*=\\frac{\\sum_i a_i}{\\sum_i a_i^2}=\\frac{3}{3.32}\\approx0.9036.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The nominal model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAcross the three training scenarios, mean error decreases from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.1067<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1067<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.0964<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.0964<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a separately considered gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsquared error with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.00711<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.00711<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrather than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.04<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhowever, it increases (approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.0768<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.0768<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.04<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nThe graphic compares the two error curves. This example does not prove\ngeneral robustness: it optimises an average, not the maximum error.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.4<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>a.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>a)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>loss<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> a,u: np.mean((a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(u,<span class=\"dv\">75<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">83<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> loss(a,u)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>loss(a,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> loss(np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>]),u)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.04<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> loss(np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>]),u)<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.04<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(u,loss(a,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>),loss(a,u),loss(np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>]),u))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transfer and evidence<\/h2>\n<p>Realistic randomisation requires plausible parameter ranges and\nrelationships. Combining mass, friction and motor power in physically\nimpossible ways may encourage inappropriate strategies. System\nidentification or real calibration data can improve the simulation\ndistribution. A staged evaluation begins with previously unseen\nsimulated scenarios, uses hardware-in-the-loop where appropriate, and\nproceeds to controlled physical trials only within safeguarded operating\nlimits. Execution time, peak energy demand, failure rates and rare\nscenarios should be recorded alongside mean rewards. Distribution shift\nand contact phenomena missing from the model remain possible even after\nsuccessful randomisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Which constant input minimises mean squared\nerror when training uses only the equally weighted gains 0.5 and\n1.5?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^*=(0.5+1.5)/(0.25+2.25)=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mean error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">((0.4-1)^2+(1.2-1)^2)/2=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncompared with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.25<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn individual state may still exhibit a larger error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.06907\">Tobin et al. (2017):\nDomain Randomization for Transferring Deep Neural Networks from\nSimulation to the Real World<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://underactuated.mit.edu/\">Tedrake: Underactuated\nRobotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · contents overview of Policy Search and Feedback\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M29-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M29-L6 – Safety Limits and Evaluation of Autonomous Systems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Robotics and Autonomous Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/applications<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18, M22, M25.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model operational limits and\nstopping distance, and assess what can be inferred from a finite number\nof safety trials.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Autonomy under defined conditions<\/h2>\n<p>An autonomous trolley may operate only within a defined operational\ndomain. This includes floor conditions, gradients, lighting, permitted\nspeed, visibility, communication conditions and proximity to people.\nSuch limits are part of the safety argument. Hazards arise not only from\ncollisions, but also from unexpected movement, crushing or trapping,\nfalling loads and failure to provide assistance. Risk assessment must\nconsider the entire operation and possible malfunctions. A learned\nperception system and an independent mechanism for detecting hazardous\nstates can have different roles. An emergency stop is an additional\nmeasure, not a substitute for safe design. The calculation below is a\nsynthetic teaching example only; it is neither a design specification\nnor an approval for actual machinery.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reaction distance and braking distance<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001692_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Reaction, braking, and safety reserve distances are stacked to total 1.4875 metres at 1.5 metres per second.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Reaction, braking, and safety reserve\ndistances are stacked to total 1.4875 metres at 1.5 metres per\nsecond.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Idealized stopping distance of a robot.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A vehicle travels on level ground at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe simplified model assumes a maximum total reaction delay\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=0.2\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollowed by constant deceleration of magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1.2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSpeed remains constant during the reaction delay. With an additional\nmargin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.25\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(v)=v\\tau+\\frac{v^2}{2a}+b.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Reaction distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.30\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbraking distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9375<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.9375\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand total distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.4875<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.4875\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic displays these components for different speeds. The model\ndoes not directly cover moving obstacles, gradients, changing friction,\nacceleration during the delay or variable braking force. Real\napplications require substantiated conservative bounds, measurement\nuncertainties, and the reaction and approach movements of everyone\ninvolved.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> sqrt,log<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v,tau,a,b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>stop<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> v: v<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>tau<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>v<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>v<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(stop(v)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.4875<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>vmax<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>tau<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>sqrt((a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>tau)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(stop(vmax)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>upper<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.029<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>upper<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.030<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(stop(v),vmax,upper)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario-based evaluation<\/h2>\n<p>A test plan defines scenarios, measurements, abort conditions and\nacceptance criteria before execution. In addition to task success, it\nshould assess collisions, near misses, minimum clearances, localisation\nerrors, failure responses and timing behaviour. NIST describes\nrepeatable procedures for quantitatively assessing different\ncapabilities of response robots. These provide a model for systematic\ntesting, not a general certification standard for this teaching example.\nObserving zero failures does not imply zero risk: in 100 independent,\nidentically distributed Bernoulli trials with no failures, the exact\none-sided 95% upper confidence bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.05<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0295<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_U=1-0.05^{1/100}\\approx0.0295<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-p_U)^{100}=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis interpretation is not justified for dependent repetitions or\nunrepresentative scenarios. Extremely rare hazards cannot be excluded\nthrough short test series alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> What speed corresponds to only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof available stopping distance in the idealised model above?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> The positive root of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\tau+v^2/(2a)=1-b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.123<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=-a\\tau+\\sqrt{(a\\tau)^2+2a(1-b)}\\approx1.123\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a mathematical model limit, not a permissible real-world speed.\nApproving a real system requires further evidence and compliance with\nthe applicable requirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/el/intelligent-systems-division-73500/standard-test-methods-response-robots\">NIST:\nStandard Test Methods for Response Robots<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · project description and\ntest goals opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/nu-gm-book-resource/\">Lynch\nand Park (2017): Modern Robotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · chapter overview and descriptions\nfor Chapters 2, 3, 10 and 11 opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://underactuated.mit.edu/\">Tedrake: Underactuated\nRobotics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · contents overview of Policy Search and Feedback\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
